## 📖 Problem Statement

Given a sorted array that has been **rotated**, search for a given `target`.

Unlike **33. Search in Rotated Sorted Array**, this problem allows **duplicate values**.

Return:

```text
true

if the target exists, otherwise:

false

The goal is to use a modified Binary Search approach.

💡 Observation

This problem is almost the same as:

33. Search in Rotated Sorted Array

The important difference is:

Duplicates are allowed.

Because of duplicates, sometimes we cannot determine which half is sorted.

For example:

[1, 0, 1, 1, 1]

Suppose:

left = 0
mid = 2
right = 4

Then:

nums[left] = nums[mid] = nums[right] = 1

We cannot tell whether the left half or right half is the sorted half.

⚠️ Special Case : Duplicates

When:

nums[left] == nums[mid] && nums[mid] == nums[right]

we cannot determine which side is sorted.

So safely remove the duplicate boundary elements:

left++;
right--;

This does not remove a possible unique target because:

nums[left] == nums[mid] == nums[right]

and we already checked:

if(nums[mid] == target)

Therefore, if target were equal to those values, we would already have returned true.

🧠 How It Works

At every iteration:

Step 1: Check the middle
if(nums[mid] == target)
    return true;
Step 2: Handle duplicates

If:

nums[left] == nums[mid] && nums[mid] == nums[right]

we cannot identify the sorted half.

So:

left++;
right--;
Step 3: Identify the sorted half

If:

nums[left] <= nums[mid]

then the left half is sorted.

Otherwise:

right half is sorted
🔍 Case 1 : Left Half is Sorted

If:

nums[left] <= nums[mid]

then check whether the target lies inside the sorted left half:

nums[left] <= target < nums[mid]

If yes:

right = mid - 1;

Otherwise:

left = mid + 1;
🔍 Case 2 : Right Half is Sorted

Otherwise, the right half is sorted.

Check whether:

nums[mid] < target <= nums[right]

If yes:

left = mid + 1;

Otherwise:

right = mid - 1;
