⚠️ Important Difference from Problem 33
Problem 33

All values are distinct.

Therefore, we can always identify the sorted half.

Time = O(log n)
Problem 81

Duplicates are allowed.

Sometimes:

nums[left] == nums[mid] == nums[right]

and we have to shrink the search space using:

left++;
right--;

In the worst case, this can reduce the search space by only two elements at a time.

Therefore:

Worst-case Time = O(n)

But in typical cases where duplicates do not prevent us from identifying the sorted half:

Average / typical Time ≈ O(log n)
✅ Approach : Modified Binary Search
Time Complexity
Average: O(log n)

Worst Case: O(n)

The worst case occurs because duplicates can prevent us from identifying which half is sorted.

Space Complexity
O(1)
Code
class Solution {

    public boolean search(int[] nums, int target) {

        int n=nums.length;
        int left=0;
        int right=n-1;

        while(left<=right){

            int mid=left+(right-left)/2;

            if(nums[mid]==target)
                return true;

            // Special case for handling duplicates
            // Cannot determine which side is sorted
            if(nums[left]==nums[mid] && nums[right]==nums[mid]){
                left++;
                right--;
                continue;
            }

            // Left side is sorted
            if(nums[left]<=nums[mid]){

                if(nums[left]<=target && target<nums[mid]){
                    right=mid-1; // search left space
                }else{
                    left=mid+1; // search right space
                }

            }

            // Right side is sorted
            else{

                if(target<=nums[right] && target>nums[mid]){
                    left=mid+1; // search right space
                }else{
                    right=mid-1; // search left space
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
