Approach	Time Complexity	Space Complexity
Modified Binary Search	O(log n) average	O(1)
Modified Binary Search	O(n) worst case	O(1)
🎯 Key Takeaways
This is a modified Binary Search problem.
At least one half is normally sorted.
Use the sorted half to decide where the target can exist.
Duplicates are the special problem.
When:
nums[left] == nums[mid] && nums[mid] == nums[right]

we cannot determine the sorted half.

In that situation:
left++;
right--;
Because duplicates can force us to shrink the array one element at a time, the worst-case complexity becomes:
O(n)
This is the key difference between 33. Search in Rotated Sorted Array and 81. Search in Rotated Sorted Array II.

